1
00:00:02,373 --> 00:00:06,013
So that are trees and we saw some examples.

2
00:00:06,205 --> 00:00:09,412
Now before we write some example code that

3
00:00:09,717 --> 00:00:12,619
actually reflects a tree or that allows us

4
00:00:12,619 --> 00:00:14,768
to work with a tree data structure,

5
00:00:15,425 --> 00:00:18,536
there is more terminology which I wanna get

6
00:00:18,616 --> 00:00:19,194
out of the way.

7
00:00:20,156 --> 00:00:23,475
We already learned about the node or vertex

8
00:00:23,523 --> 00:00:25,992
which is simply a structure in a tree that

9
00:00:25,992 --> 00:00:29,073
contains a value. So that bubble in the tree

10
00:00:29,202 --> 00:00:31,594
which contains the actual value, for

11
00:00:31,627 --> 00:00:35,015
example, the HTML tag or the decision.

12
00:00:36,123 --> 00:00:39,061
Now those nodes are connected with edges,

13
00:00:39,575 --> 00:00:42,353
which, well, are simply the connections

14
00:00:42,465 --> 00:00:45,484
between 2 nodes. And an edge is always

15
00:00:45,596 --> 00:00:49,466
between exactly 2 nodes, not between 3 or 4,

16
00:00:50,076 --> 00:00:53,063
and also not around one node. It's always a

17
00:00:53,063 --> 00:00:55,841
connection between exactly 2 nodes. Nodes.

18
00:00:56,757 --> 00:00:58,973
We got this root node, as you learned,

19
00:00:59,166 --> 00:01:01,206
that's the topmost node in the tree.

20
00:01:02,121 --> 00:01:03,792
And then we got a couple of other terms

21
00:01:03,888 --> 00:01:05,060
which we haven't heard yet.

22
00:01:05,590 --> 00:01:06,361
For example,

23
00:01:07,020 --> 00:01:10,216
in a tree, we can also find subtrees,

24
00:01:10,794 --> 00:01:13,380
which are nested trees inside of the tree

25
00:01:13,766 --> 00:01:16,512
where the root node of the subtree is not

26
00:01:16,512 --> 00:01:18,440
the root node of the overall tree.

27
00:01:19,484 --> 00:01:22,518
Here's an example for a subtree. The part in

28
00:01:22,518 --> 00:01:25,244
the orange box, that's a subtree of the

29
00:01:25,324 --> 00:01:26,206
overall tree.

30
00:01:27,345 --> 00:01:30,280
Another important term is the leaf.

31
00:01:30,954 --> 00:01:34,530
What's a leaf in a tree? A leaf is a node

32
00:01:34,626 --> 00:01:37,625
without any further child nodes. So you

33
00:01:37,625 --> 00:01:40,143
could say it's the end of a tree. So it

34
00:01:40,175 --> 00:01:41,859
doesn't have its own subtree.

35
00:01:42,581 --> 00:01:45,596
If we take this example tree here, we would

36
00:01:45,628 --> 00:01:49,605
have 5 leaves because we got 5 nodes without

37
00:01:49,701 --> 00:01:51,321
any further child nodes.

38
00:01:53,084 --> 00:01:56,625
We also have the concept of paths inside of

39
00:01:56,625 --> 00:02:00,183
a tree. Now edges are connections between 2

40
00:02:00,279 --> 00:02:00,663
nodes.

41
00:02:01,256 --> 00:02:04,397
Paths are now simply a sequence of nodes and

42
00:02:04,525 --> 00:02:07,570
edges that connect 2 nodes which are not

43
00:02:07,570 --> 00:02:10,454
necessarily in a direct connection to each

44
00:02:10,454 --> 00:02:10,630
other.

45
00:02:11,432 --> 00:02:12,377
Here's an example.

46
00:02:13,034 --> 00:02:14,236
These 2 nodes here,

47
00:02:14,797 --> 00:02:17,473
they have a path that connects them.

48
00:02:18,146 --> 00:02:22,540
And it's this path of 2 edges and 3 nodes

49
00:02:22,732 --> 00:02:23,117
overall.

50
00:02:24,239 --> 00:02:26,549
You also see that I'm already introducing

51
00:02:26,581 --> 00:02:29,243
another term here, and that's the distance

52
00:02:29,387 --> 00:02:32,434
between 2 nodes. The distance is the number

53
00:02:32,434 --> 00:02:34,310
of edges we need overall

54
00:02:34,855 --> 00:02:36,250
to connect 2 nodes,

55
00:02:36,828 --> 00:02:39,137
even if there is another node in between.

56
00:02:39,826 --> 00:02:43,097
So the path between these 2 nodes, which are

57
00:02:43,097 --> 00:02:46,080
marked with the arrows, that path would have

58
00:02:46,080 --> 00:02:46,913
a distance of 2.

59
00:02:47,731 --> 00:02:50,119
Or put in other words, the distance between

60
00:02:50,232 --> 00:02:53,373
these 2 nodes with the arrows is 2.

61
00:02:54,255 --> 00:02:57,060
So distance is another term you should have

62
00:02:57,108 --> 00:02:59,337
heard about when you're talking about trees.

63
00:03:00,667 --> 00:03:02,783
Now another concept which I already

64
00:03:03,136 --> 00:03:05,444
mentioned actually is the parent-child

65
00:03:05,444 --> 00:03:08,618
concept. That's a core part of a tree.

66
00:03:09,452 --> 00:03:11,151
We have multiple nodes in a tree

67
00:03:11,776 --> 00:03:14,084
and multiple nodes are always in some

68
00:03:14,341 --> 00:03:16,809
relation and all those nodes are indeed

69
00:03:16,810 --> 00:03:19,090
different kinds of relations to each other.

70
00:03:19,716 --> 00:03:22,350
For example, we speak of parent and child

71
00:03:22,366 --> 00:03:25,305
nodes if 2 nodes are directly connected with

72
00:03:25,337 --> 00:03:25,754
each other.

73
00:03:26,557 --> 00:03:26,814
The

74
00:03:27,344 --> 00:03:29,641
node higher up in the tree is then the

75
00:03:29,753 --> 00:03:32,627
parent node of the node below it, which

76
00:03:32,740 --> 00:03:33,992
would then be the child node.

77
00:03:34,908 --> 00:03:37,959
We also have ancestors and descendants,

78
00:03:38,505 --> 00:03:40,400
and that simply are nodes which are

79
00:03:40,593 --> 00:03:43,226
indirectly connected. So if there are nodes

80
00:03:43,339 --> 00:03:43,949
in between,

81
00:03:44,526 --> 00:03:47,074
Then a node on a higher level would be an

82
00:03:47,250 --> 00:03:47,763
ancestor.

83
00:03:48,308 --> 00:03:50,087
A node on a lower level would be a

84
00:03:50,087 --> 00:03:50,680
descendant.

85
00:03:51,433 --> 00:03:53,804
We also have sibling nodes, which are nodes

86
00:03:53,917 --> 00:03:56,481
on the same level which are not directly

87
00:03:56,481 --> 00:03:58,740
connected to each other, but which have the

88
00:03:58,740 --> 00:04:00,134
same parent node.

89
00:04:00,887 --> 00:04:03,083
And to make those concepts clearer again,

90
00:04:03,692 --> 00:04:05,086
in this example tree,

91
00:04:05,615 --> 00:04:08,563
the parent node for this node in the bottom

92
00:04:08,579 --> 00:04:10,790
right corner would be this node. This node.

93
00:04:11,239 --> 00:04:13,883
So this here would be the child node of this

94
00:04:14,107 --> 00:04:14,844
parent node.

95
00:04:15,709 --> 00:04:18,897
In addition, if we now add this very node at

96
00:04:18,897 --> 00:04:19,666
the top here,

97
00:04:20,211 --> 00:04:23,768
then that node is not only the root node of

98
00:04:23,864 --> 00:04:24,377
the tree,

99
00:04:24,922 --> 00:04:26,972
of course it is, but it's not just that,

100
00:04:27,389 --> 00:04:31,074
it's also an ancestor to the child node and

101
00:04:31,074 --> 00:04:33,653
a parent to the parent node in the middle

102
00:04:33,653 --> 00:04:33,814
here.

103
00:04:34,615 --> 00:04:37,258
Now if we look at another node here, let's

104
00:04:37,258 --> 00:04:39,309
say the node in the bottom left corner,

105
00:04:39,887 --> 00:04:43,018
That node would be a descendant of that root

106
00:04:43,018 --> 00:04:46,133
level node, for example. It's not a direct

107
00:04:46,229 --> 00:04:48,413
child because there is a node in between,

108
00:04:48,878 --> 00:04:50,982
but it is on a lower level and therefore a

109
00:04:50,982 --> 00:04:51,640
descendant.

110
00:04:52,652 --> 00:04:55,879
The node next to that bottom left node here

111
00:04:56,361 --> 00:04:58,561
would, for example, be a sibling to that

112
00:04:58,753 --> 00:04:59,701
descendant node.

113
00:05:00,375 --> 00:05:02,832
So these are a couple of other important

114
00:05:02,864 --> 00:05:04,261
terms which you should be aware of.

115
00:05:05,627 --> 00:05:08,020
Now there is more terminology, but not much,

116
00:05:08,295 --> 00:05:08,715
no worries.

117
00:05:09,329 --> 00:05:12,628
For example, when we look at a tree, we also

118
00:05:12,644 --> 00:05:14,276
have a concept called degree.

119
00:05:15,082 --> 00:05:15,986
The degree

120
00:05:16,518 --> 00:05:20,261
of a node is the number of child nodes of a

121
00:05:20,325 --> 00:05:20,938
given node.

122
00:05:22,568 --> 00:05:25,749
We also have the level of a node. That's the

123
00:05:25,894 --> 00:05:28,447
distance between a node and the root node.

124
00:05:28,899 --> 00:05:31,253
So the distance, a concept we already

125
00:05:31,269 --> 00:05:33,521
learned about, that is something we can have

126
00:05:33,618 --> 00:05:34,979
between any 2 nodes,

127
00:05:35,967 --> 00:05:38,227
But the distance between a node and the root

128
00:05:38,227 --> 00:05:42,251
node is always also called the level of that

129
00:05:42,459 --> 00:05:44,687
other node, so of the non-root node.

130
00:05:46,146 --> 00:05:47,909
We also have something which is called the

131
00:05:48,086 --> 00:05:50,827
depth of a tree, and that's the maximum

132
00:05:51,035 --> 00:05:53,648
level in a tree. It simply describes how

133
00:05:53,921 --> 00:05:55,427
deeply nested a tree is.

134
00:05:56,341 --> 00:05:58,361
We also got the breadth of a tree, and

135
00:05:58,409 --> 00:06:00,541
that's the number of leaves in a tree,

136
00:06:00,733 --> 00:06:04,340
because this describes how broad it is if we

137
00:06:04,436 --> 00:06:06,248
would draw it on a piece of paper, for

138
00:06:06,280 --> 00:06:08,028
example. That's how you can think of that.

139
00:06:08,446 --> 00:06:11,575
Depth is simply how much vertical space you

140
00:06:11,575 --> 00:06:14,769
need. Breadth is how much horizontal space

141
00:06:14,769 --> 00:06:15,074
you need.

142
00:06:15,989 --> 00:06:17,995
And last but not least, we got the size of a

143
00:06:17,995 --> 00:06:20,531
tree, and that's the total number of nodes

144
00:06:20,788 --> 00:06:21,077
overall,

145
00:06:21,606 --> 00:06:24,126
no matter where they are and how they are

146
00:06:24,254 --> 00:06:24,720
connected.

147
00:06:25,394 --> 00:06:27,063
Now let's have a look at all those concepts

148
00:06:27,063 --> 00:06:27,352
again.

149
00:06:28,010 --> 00:06:29,807
For this tree here, for example,

150
00:06:30,337 --> 00:06:32,504
we could note the degrees of the different

151
00:06:32,504 --> 00:06:32,889
nodes.

152
00:06:34,028 --> 00:06:36,193
And keep in mind that the degree of a node

153
00:06:36,209 --> 00:06:39,256
is the number of child nodes, of direct

154
00:06:39,256 --> 00:06:42,528
child nodes, of that given node. So for the

155
00:06:42,528 --> 00:06:44,596
root level node, we would have a degree of 2

156
00:06:44,933 --> 00:06:47,724
because this has 2 direct child nodes.

157
00:06:48,301 --> 00:06:50,739
For the other nodes here, these would be the

158
00:06:50,739 --> 00:06:53,898
degrees. Please note that the node in the

159
00:06:53,898 --> 00:06:56,448
middle on the right has a degree of 3

160
00:06:56,496 --> 00:06:58,629
because it has 3 child nodes.

161
00:06:59,287 --> 00:07:01,311
All the leaves in the bottom row have a

162
00:07:01,311 --> 00:07:03,945
degree of 0 because they have no child

163
00:07:03,945 --> 00:07:04,218
nodes.

164
00:07:05,246 --> 00:07:07,415
We also had that concept of a

165
00:07:07,961 --> 00:07:08,346
level,

166
00:07:08,924 --> 00:07:10,772
which was the distance between a node and

167
00:07:10,852 --> 00:07:11,559
the root node.

168
00:07:12,860 --> 00:07:16,024
In this tree here, the level of the root

169
00:07:16,072 --> 00:07:19,188
node would be 0 because the distance between

170
00:07:19,284 --> 00:07:21,758
that node and the root node, since that is

171
00:07:21,790 --> 00:07:23,637
the root node, is of course 0.

172
00:07:24,360 --> 00:07:26,335
But here are the distances for the other

173
00:07:26,448 --> 00:07:29,019
nodes in the tree. For all the leaf nodes at

174
00:07:29,019 --> 00:07:31,317
the very bottom, the distance is 2 because

175
00:07:31,349 --> 00:07:34,354
they all have a path of distance 2. They all

176
00:07:34,467 --> 00:07:38,197
have 2 edges between themselves and the root

177
00:07:38,197 --> 00:07:38,457
node.

178
00:07:40,180 --> 00:07:43,148
This tree overall would have a depth of 2

179
00:07:43,664 --> 00:07:46,117
because as you learned, the depth of a tree

180
00:07:46,568 --> 00:07:48,666
is the maximum level in a tree.

181
00:07:49,246 --> 00:07:51,507
Let's also already memorize that the breadth

182
00:07:51,771 --> 00:07:52,990
was the number of leaves.

183
00:07:54,048 --> 00:07:56,328
And with that in mind, it makes sense that

184
00:07:56,376 --> 00:07:59,347
this tree has a depth of 2 because the

185
00:07:59,347 --> 00:08:02,461
maximum level here is 2 for all the bottom

186
00:08:02,574 --> 00:08:03,762
leaves, for example.

187
00:08:04,485 --> 00:08:07,118
And the breadth of this tree would be 5

188
00:08:07,150 --> 00:08:09,045
because we have 5 leaves.

189
00:08:09,639 --> 00:08:12,208
Again, breadth is basically describing how

190
00:08:12,208 --> 00:08:14,825
much space we need horizontally and depth

191
00:08:14,937 --> 00:08:17,490
describes how much space we need vertically,

192
00:08:17,507 --> 00:08:18,020
you could say.

193
00:08:20,139 --> 00:08:22,609
Now, this tree overall would have a size of

194
00:08:22,866 --> 00:08:25,577
8 if we have a look at that size property

195
00:08:26,010 --> 00:08:28,369
because the size describes the overall

196
00:08:28,561 --> 00:08:30,454
number of nodes in a tree.

197
00:08:31,609 --> 00:08:34,561
And that's it for the core terms. Sounds

198
00:08:34,657 --> 00:08:37,096
like a lot but actually it's not that hard

199
00:08:37,144 --> 00:08:40,144
to memorize. A lot of those concepts are

200
00:08:40,353 --> 00:08:43,930
related. You also find those slides with

201
00:08:43,962 --> 00:08:46,295
those terms attached And in general, the

202
00:08:46,295 --> 00:08:48,990
most important part is that you generally

203
00:08:49,136 --> 00:08:52,348
understand what a tree is and how it works.

204
00:08:53,090 --> 00:08:55,237
And for that, let's now also dive in some

205
00:08:55,350 --> 00:08:55,721
code.
